---Provides details about an Amazon MQ message broker. A message broker allows software applications and components to communicate using various programming languages, operating systems, and formal messaging protocols.
---@class aws.securityhub.AwsAmazonMqBrokerDetails
---@field AuthenticationStrategy? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyString The authentication strategy used to secure the broker. The default is SIMPLE.
---@field AutoMinorVersionUpgrade? aws.securityhub.Boolean Whether automatically upgrade new minor versions for brokers, as new versions are released and supported by Amazon MQ. Automatic upgrades occur during the scheduled maintenance window of the broker or after a manual broker reboot.
---@field BrokerArn? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyString The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the broker.
---@field BrokerName? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyString The broker's name.
---@field DeploymentMode? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyString The broker's deployment mode.
---@field EncryptionOptions? aws.securityhub.AwsAmazonMqBrokerEncryptionOptionsDetails Encryption options for the broker. Doesn’t apply to RabbitMQ brokers.
---@field EngineType? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyString The type of broker engine.
---@field EngineVersion? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyString The version of the broker engine.
---@field HostInstanceType? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyString The broker's instance type.
---@field BrokerId? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyString The unique ID that Amazon MQ generates for the broker.
---@field LdapServerMetadata? aws.securityhub.AwsAmazonMqBrokerLdapServerMetadataDetails The metadata of the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) server used to authenticate and authorize connections to the broker. This is an optional failover server.
---@field Logs? aws.securityhub.AwsAmazonMqBrokerLogsDetails Turns on Amazon CloudWatch logging for brokers.
---@field MaintenanceWindowStartTime? aws.securityhub.AwsAmazonMqBrokerMaintenanceWindowStartTimeDetails The scheduled time period (UTC) during which Amazon MQ begins to apply pending updates or patches to the broker.
---@field PubliclyAccessible? aws.securityhub.Boolean Permits connections from applications outside of the VPC that hosts the broker's subnets.
---@field SecurityGroups? aws.securityhub.StringList The list of rules (one minimum, 125 maximum) that authorize connections to brokers.
---@field StorageType? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyString The broker's storage type.
---@field SubnetIds? aws.securityhub.StringList The list of groups that define which subnets and IP ranges the broker can use from different Availability Zones.
---@field Users? aws.securityhub.AwsAmazonMqBrokerUsersList The list of all broker usernames for the specified broker. Doesn't apply to RabbitMQ brokers.

---Provides details about broker encryption options.
---@class aws.securityhub.AwsAmazonMqBrokerEncryptionOptionsDetails
---@field KmsKeyId? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyString The KMS key that’s used to encrypt your data at rest. If not provided, Amazon MQ will use a default KMS key to encrypt your data.
---@field UseAwsOwnedKey? aws.securityhub.Boolean Specifies that an KMS key should be used for at-rest encryption. Set to true by default if no value is provided (for example, for RabbitMQ brokers).

---The metadata of the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) server used to authenticate and authorize connections to the broker. This is an optional failover server.
---@class aws.securityhub.AwsAmazonMqBrokerLdapServerMetadataDetails
---@field Hosts? aws.securityhub.StringList Specifies the location of the LDAP server, such as Amazon Web Services Directory Service for Microsoft Active Directory.
---@field RoleBase? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyString The distinguished name of the node in the directory information tree (DIT) to search for roles or groups.
---@field RoleName? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyString The group name attribute in a role entry whose value is the name of that role.
---@field RoleSearchMatching? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyString The LDAP search filter used to find roles within the roleBase.
---@field RoleSearchSubtree? aws.securityhub.Boolean The directory search scope for the role. If set to true, the scope is to search the entire subtree.
---@field ServiceAccountUsername? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyString A username for the service account, which is an account in your LDAP server that has access to initiate a connection.
---@field UserBase? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyString Selects a particular subtree of the directory information tree (DIT) to search for user entries.
---@field UserRoleName? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyString The name of the LDAP attribute in the user's directory entry for the user's group membership.
---@field UserSearchMatching? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyString The LDAP search filter used to find users within the userBase.
---@field UserSearchSubtree? aws.securityhub.Boolean The directory search scope for the user. If set to true, the scope is to search the entire subtree.

---Provides information about logs to be activated for the specified broker.
