---@field Informational aws.securityhub.TrendsValueCount The count of findings with Informational severity level at this point in the trend timeline.
---@field Low aws.securityhub.TrendsValueCount The count of findings with Low severity level at this point in the trend timeline.
---@field Medium aws.securityhub.TrendsValueCount The count of findings with Medium severity level at this point in the trend timeline.
---@field High aws.securityhub.TrendsValueCount The count of findings with High severity level at this point in the trend timeline.
---@field Critical aws.securityhub.TrendsValueCount The count of findings with Critical severity level at this point in the trend timeline.
---@field Fatal aws.securityhub.TrendsValueCount The count of findings with Fatal severity level at this point in the trend timeline.
---@field Other aws.securityhub.TrendsValueCount The count of findings with severity levels not fitting into the standard categories at this point in the trend timeline.

---Updates to the severity information for a finding.
---@class aws.securityhub.SeverityUpdate
---@field Normalized? aws.securityhub.RatioScale The normalized severity for the finding. This attribute is to be deprecated in favor of Label.
---@field Product? aws.securityhub.Double The native severity as defined by the Amazon Web Services service or integrated partner product that generated the finding.
---@field Label? aws.securityhub.SeverityLabel|string The severity value of the finding. The allowed values are the following.

---Contains information about the signals involved in an Amazon GuardDuty Extended Threat Detection attack sequence. An attack sequence is a type of threat detected by GuardDuty. GuardDuty generates an attack sequence finding when multiple events, or signals, align to a potentially suspicious activity. When GuardDuty and Security Hub CSPM are integrated, GuardDuty sends attack sequence findings to Se...
---@class aws.securityhub.Signal
---@field Type? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyString The type of the signal used to identify an attack sequence.
---@field Id? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyString The identifier of the signal.
---@field Title? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyString The description of the GuardDuty finding.
---@field ProductArn? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyString The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the product that generated the signal.
---@field ResourceIds? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyStringList The ARN or ID of the Amazon Web Services resource associated with the signal.
---@field SignalIndicators? aws.securityhub.IndicatorsList Contains information about the indicators associated with the signals in this attack sequence finding. The values for SignalIndicators are a subset of the values for SequenceIndicators, but the values for these fields don't always match 1:1.
---@field Name? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyString The name of the GuardDuty signal. For example, when signal type is FINDING, the signal name is the name of the finding.
---@field CreatedAt? aws.securityhub.Long The timestamp when the first finding or activity related to this signal was observed.
---@field UpdatedAt? aws.securityhub.Long The timestamp when this signal was last observed.
---@field FirstSeenAt? aws.securityhub.Long The timestamp when the first finding or activity related to this signal was observed.
---@field LastSeenAt? aws.securityhub.Long The timestamp when the last finding or activity related to this signal was observed.
---@field Severity? aws.securityhub.Double The severity associated with the signal. For more information about severity, see Severity levels for GuardDuty findings in the Amazon GuardDuty User Guide.
---@field Count? aws.securityhub.Integer The number of times this signal was observed.
---@field ActorIds? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyStringList The IDs of the threat actors involved in the signal.
---@field EndpointIds? aws.securityhub.NonEmptyStringList Information about the endpoint IDs associated with this signal.

---@alias aws.securityhub.SignalsList aws.securityhub.Signal[]

